  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "From **top to bottom**, the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ intersects the line $x=-5$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=-5,$ we substitute $x=-5$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-5)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1-\\frac{25}{16} \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm 1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $x=-5$  at the points $(-5,1.5)$ and $(-5,-1.5).$",
